<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Xiao Tong</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">501–<a href="/years/531">531</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> · Also known as Crown Prince Zhaoming</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Xiao Tong, known posthumously as Crown Prince Zhaoming, was the eldest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-han">Emperor Wu</a> of the Liang dynasty in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. A devout Buddhist and patron of letters, he gathered scholars at his court and directed the compilation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wen-xuan">Wen Xuan</a>, the most influential anthology of early Chinese literature. He died before his father, after an accident while boating, and never reigned.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/xiao-tong.jpg" alt="Xiao Tong" width="268" height="414" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wang Qi, Wang Siyiji (王圻 王思義輯) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E6%98%AD%E6%98%8E%E5%A4%AA%E5%AD%90%E5%83%8F.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Xiao Tong was born in the winter of 501 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiangyang">Xiangyang</a> to Xiao Yan, then a Southern Qi general close to victory in a civil war, and his concubine Ding Lingguang. In 502 Xiao Yan took the throne as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-liang">Emperor Wu of Liang</a>, and on 24 December 502, when Xiao Tong was one year old, he made him crown prince. His mother, though not made empress, was given a status equal to her son's. He married Lady Cai on 17 May 508 and was declared an adult on 31 January 515.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Like his father he became a devout Buddhist, studying the sutras and inviting monks to preach at his palace, and Emperor Wu gave him more and more matters of state. When his uncle Xiao Dan died in 522, he held that the custom sparing a crown prince from mourning an uncle was unfilial, and a one-month mourning set a precedent for the dynasty. With others he compiled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wen-xuan">Wen Xuan</a>, an anthology in 60 chapters whose 29th preserves the Nineteen Old Poems, a major source for early classical Chinese poetry.</p><p>His mother died on 3 January 527. When a Taoist monk warned that her burial ground would bring ill fortune to her eldest son, Xiao Tong let him bury wax geese there to avert it. An attendant later reported this to Emperor Wu as sorcery. The emperor executed only the monk, on the advice of the prime minister Xu Mian, but Xiao Tong never fully regained his father's trust.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Xiao Tong died in May 531, writing submissions to his father even while gravely ill. Emperor Wu buried him with an emperor's honours but, still resentful over the wax geese, passed over his son Xiao Huan and made his younger brother Xiao Gang crown prince. In 551 the rebel general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hou-jing">Hou Jing</a> deposed Xiao Gang and enthroned Xiao Tong's grandson Xiao Dong, and Xiao Tong was then honoured posthumously as an emperor. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiao-cha">Xiao Cha</a> later reigned as Emperor Xuan.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Xiao Tong” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Emperor Wu, founder of the Liang dynasty, made his one-year-old son <b>Xiao Tong</b> crown prince, having at first declined because the empire was not yet pacified.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-24">24 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>His son <b>Xiao Tong</b> compiled the Wenxuan, the classic anthology of Chinese literature.</blockquote><a href="/state/liang-dynasty">Liang dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
